Academic Professionals Submenu Academic Professionals Incoming Academic Professionals Benefits for Academic Professionals Academic Professional Career Ranks & Advancement Research for Academic Professionals Professional Development for Academic Professionals Checklist for Departure Link downloads document Information About Postdoc and Associate Research Scholar Unionization Postdoctoral Scholars Link is external Visitors Visitors Submenu Visitors Incoming Visitors Visitor Ranks & Appointments Visitor Benefits Academic Units Academic Units Submenu Academic Units Directory of Academic Units, Chairs, Directors & Managers Guidebook for Chairs, Directors and Managers Guidelines for Academic Reviews Systems Guide & How-To’s Systems Guide & How-To’s Submenu Interfolio Faculty Search Authorization Request Guide AHIRE Guide DOF DropBox Instructions Salary Commitment Guidelines Salary Review Process Guide Faculty Roles Administration Guide New Jersey Pay Transparency Act Forms and Data Submission Forms and Data Submission Submenu Appointment and Promotion Checklists List of Current Systems Helpful Tools Calendar of Major Actions and Important Dates News Benefits Apply FAQs iThenticate FAQs Search Search Home About Section Menu Dean's Welcome Meet Our Team Whom to Contact Facts & Figures Our History and Past Deans Endowed Professorships, Preceptorships & Fellowships Emeriti Faculty Bios President's Award for Distinguished Teaching Visiting Professorships for Distinguished Teaching Howard T. Behrman Award for Distinguished Achievement in the Humanities Meet The Faculty , (current page) Public Lectures Link is external Meet The Faculty “Meet the Faculty” is produced by the Office of the Dean of the Faculty to showcase junior faculty members across disciplines and to highlight excellence in research and teaching at Princeton. In these Q&As, faculty members share — in their own words — the many ways in which their passionate pursuit of knowledge invigorates their work at the University, and how, in turn, Princeton supports their career success. Meet Ellora Derenoncourt: Illuminating inequality through curiosity-driven research In this Q&A, she reflects on her unconventional path to economics, her passion for curiosity-driven research, and her commitment to economics to confront some of society’s most enduring challenges. Meet Marissa Weichman: Cultivating the next generation of physical chemists in the lab In this Q&A, she shares her journey to academia, the challenges of starting her own lab, and her commitment to fostering a supportive environment for the next generation of scientists. Meet Jerelle Joseph: Paying it forward as a scholar in STEM In this Q&A, Joseph shares her journey pursuing pioneering research that has the potential to transform our understanding of disease progression and to open new avenues for therapeutic development. Meet Ricardo Mallarino: Decoding the genetic blueprint of animal formation and evolution In this Q&A, he reflects on the sparks that ignited his scientific curiosity, the power of mentorship, and how Princeton’s cross-disciplinary culture has propelled his research into exciting, uncharted territory. Meet Peter Kelly: Revealing patterns between ancient and modern literature In this Q&A, Kelly elaborates on his work as a practicing poet and discusses how creativity and cross-disciplinary engagement drive the study of classics forward and inform our modern ways of knowing. Meet Xiaoyu Xia: Preserving the history of Chinese book traditions and typography In this Q&A, she discusses the challenges and benefits of understanding material innovations of modern Chinese literature while seeking ways to rediscover the traditions repressed by these modern interventions. Meet Benjamin Eysenbach: Exploring AI decisionmaking through reinforcement learning Benjamin Eysenbach, assistant professor of computer science, designs algorithms so that AI agents can solve problems that humans don’t know how to solve. These AI agents learn through trial and error, rather than mimicking human actions. In this Q&A, Eysenbach discusses his research and its application for the future of AI. Meet Evan Soltas: Analyzing barriers to housing construction and affordability in America’s cities Evan Soltas, assistant professor of economics and public affairs, is a public finance economist who studies housing and urban policy. His recent research examines how taxes, subsidies and regulations shape real estate development and housing prices. In this Q&A, Soltas discusses what the high cost of housing in American cities reveals about regulatory barriers to construction. Footer Office of the Dean of the Faculty Princeton University 9 Nassau Hall, Princeton, NJ 08544-5264 Phone: 609-258-3020 Fax: 609-258-2168 Contact Us Give Feedback Discrimination/Harassment Complaint Form Link opens in new window Request Data Rules & Procedures of the Faculty Rights, Rules, Responsibilities Link is external University-Wide Policies Link is external Employer Obligation Regarding Records Link downloads document, Link opens in new window Workplace Notices Link is external Diversity & Non-Discrimination Link is external Accessibility Help Link is external © 2026 The Trustees of Princeton University m"
